15", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "At the prescribed half-window of 5000, peak turnover is only 0.30 (overlap 0.70): the group's before- and after-contact sets share the same 30-node core, so no old set gives way to a new one. Edge-level diffs look like a total rewire (jaccard 0.0) only because a different subset of the reviewed group is transmitting: members 25/396/489/638/1036/1163 carry ~90 events each before t~24000 and none after, while 1244/1366/1417/1489/1729/1763 do the reverse \u2014 yet both cohorts contact the identical partner set (verified node-by-node for 1036 vs 1244 and 25 vs 1763). The sharp turnover spikes at short bandwidth coincide with an empty activity bin [23140,25139] and sparse before-side sampling, i.e. a recording gap artefact. Hub partners remain active throughout (87/58/83 events across thirds). Verdict: internal membership handover plus a gap, not a regime switch of interaction partners."}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**What the tools show:**\n\n1. **Step_01** (bandwidth 5000, as specified): the maximum neighbourhood-turnover score over the whole window is only **0.30** (at t\u224814140), i.e. Jaccard *overlap* \u2248 0.70 between the group's before- and after-contacts. The two contact lists share a 30-node core (65, 66, 74, 163, 232, 245, 292, 389, 422, 442, 526, 702, 848, 850, 880, 978, 986, 987, 990, 1200, 1317, 1358, 1456, 1566, 1587, 1633, 1681, 1780, 1888, 1999); the differences are a handful of one-off partners. No old\u2192new substitution.\n\n2. **The apparent \"jaccard = 0.0\"** in step_03/step_15 is an artefact of edge identity, not partner identity: the *edges* change because a different subset of the reviewed group is active, while the *partners* are identical. Step_08 (node 1036, 19140\u201324139) and step_09 (node 1244, 24140\u201329139) return **exactly the same 30 neighbours**. Step_11 (node 25, early) and step_12 (node 1763, late) likewise return the same core set.\n\n3. **Internal handover, not partner turnover:** step_05/step_06 show members 25, 396, 489, 638, 1036, 1163 carry ~90 events each before ~24140 and \u22480 after, while 1244, 1366, 1417, 1489, 1729, 1763 are \u22480 before and ~90 each after. The cohort rotates who speaks, but they all speak to the same hub set.\n\n4. **Confounder controls:** step_02 shows flat volume (~68\u201387 per bin) with a single empty bin [23140, 25139] \u2014 a data gap that mechanically inflates short-bandwidth turnover scores (step_07's spurious 1.0 spikes at t=23140/25140, where the \"before\" side had only 14 sparsely sampled contacts, all a *subset* of the after side). Step_14 confirms the counterpart hub nodes stay active in all three thirds (87/58/83), so the partners were not replaced or retired. Step_10/step_13 show these nodes are not the graph's top hubs and the graph is sparse-repeat (repeat ratio 0.16), so activity volume alone explains nothing.\n\n**Conclusion:** the group's contact set is stable across [9140, 39140]; what changes is which group members are transmitting, plus a recording gap. That is not a regime switch of interaction partners.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_25544f86\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [25, 396, 489, 638, 1036, 1163, 1244, 1366, 1417, 1489, 1729, 1763],\n  \"window\": [9140, 39140],\n  \"confidence\": 0.78,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"